'''Polímers de Balboa, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Polímers de Balboa}}), commonly known as '''PDB''', is a Balboan plastics and petrochemicals company and one of the world's largest plastics manufacturers. Headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the company operates resin production facilities at [[Badia Curta]] — adjacent to [[CIEA]]'s refinery, which supplies the petrochemical feedstocks — and manufacturing plants across the [[BTPMEU]] region. It is dual-listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: PDB) and the Ampuria Stock Exchange (APX: PDB), reflecting its position as a major supplier to the Illypnian manufacturing sector.

PDB was established in 1979 as '''Resines Nacionals de Balboa''' (RNB), a state-owned enterprise created as part of the BPP government's domestic processing chain strategy. The company's founding purpose was to convert petrochemical feedstocks from [[CIEA]]'s (then Petrolí de Balboa's) Badia Curta refinery into plastic resins and products, capturing value added domestically rather than exporting raw petrochemicals. The establishment of the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] in 1981 made the company's exports competitive, and within two decades the company had grown from a small domestic resin producer into a major global plastics manufacturer.

The company was privatised through an initial public offering on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor in 1994, as part of the broader privatisation programme that also included [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme]] (CAI). The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] retained a stake (now 8%), and CIEA acquired a 12% stake, formalising the long-standing feedstock supply relationship. A secondary listing on the Ampuria Stock Exchange followed in 2003, as the company's exports to the Illypnian manufacturing sector — particularly [[Volisania]], [[Breisland]], and [[Okaiken]] — grew rapidly and Volisanian institutional investors sought exposure to the company.

PDB's competitive position is a direct consequence of the Balboan economic model. The overvalued International Dollar makes crude oil imports cheap for CIEA, which supplies feedstocks to PDB at competitive prices; the negative tariff offsets the overvalued ID's cost penalty on exports; and the Balboa Canal and Sant Cristòfor harbour provide efficient export logistics. The company's resin production at Badia Curta also benefits from relatively low electricity costs, supplied by the hydroelectric dams of the [[Yalabirga]] range. This combination — cheap feedstocks, low energy costs, export rebates, and canal logistics — gives PDB a structural cost advantage that has made it one of the world's lowest-cost plastic resin producers.

== History ==

=== Founding and state ownership (1979–1994) ===

The BPP government's industrialisation strategy after the reunification focused on capturing value added domestically rather than exporting raw materials. While [[CIEA]] (then Petrolí de Balboa) had been refining crude oil at Badia Curta since the 1950s, the petrochemical feedstocks produced by the refinery — naphtha, ethylene, propylene — were being exported as raw materials rather than processed into higher-value plastic products. The government identified plastics manufacturing as a key sector for vertical integration: the feedstocks were available domestically, the technology was accessible, and the products could be exported with the benefit of the negative tariff.

Resines Nacionals de Balboa was established in 1979 as a state-owned enterprise under the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]]. The company built its first resin production facility at Badia Curta, adjacent to the Petrolí de Balboa refinery, with a pipeline connecting the two facilities for direct feedstock supply. The company initially produced basic plastic resins — polyethylene, polypropylene, and PVC — for the domestic market.

The establishment of the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] in 1981 transformed the company's economics. With the export rebate offsetting the overvalued ID's cost penalty, RNB's resins became competitive on the world market, and the company began exporting to BTPMEU and broader AC markets. The company expanded through the 1980s, adding resin production capacity and beginning to manufacture finished plastic products — packaging, components, and industrial materials — alongside its resin output.

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] of the late 1980s and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]] had a limited direct impact on RNB, as the company's operations were in the industrial sector rather than real estate. However, the broader economic contraction and the government's fiscal difficulties prompted a reassessment of the state-owned enterprise sector. The BPP government, which had previously opposed privatisation on ideological grounds, concluded that RNB would benefit from private capital and management discipline.

=== Privatisation and global expansion (1994–present) ===

RNB was privatised through an IPO on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1994, renamed '''Polímers de Balboa''' as part of the privatisation. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] retained a 10% stake, and CIEA — which had been privatised in 1974 and was preparing for its 1996 merger with Sanrobonian oil company SMSS — acquired a 12% stake, formalising the long-standing feedstock supply relationship. The IPO was moderately successful, with strong interest from institutional investors attracted by the company's strategic position in the domestic processing chain.

The establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998 opened the Tapuya and Potocsí markets for PDB's products. The company initially exported to these markets from its Balboan facilities, but by the early 2000s it had begun acquiring Tapuyan and Potocsí plastics manufacturers to establish local production, taking advantage of lower labour costs in those countries for the manufacturing of finished plastic products. The resin production remained concentrated at Badia Curta, where the feedstock supply and hydropower advantages were located, but the manufacturing of packaging, components, and industrial plastics was distributed across the BTPMEU.

A secondary listing on the Ampuria Stock Exchange was completed in 2003, as PDB's exports to the Illypnian manufacturing sector grew. The company had become a major supplier of plastic components to the assembly chain — injection-moulded casings, housings, and structural parts that fed into the sub-assembly operations in Balboa and were then exported to final-assembly markets in [[Volisania]], [[Breisland]], and [[Okaiken]]. Volisanian institutional investors, recognising PDB's role in their manufacturing supply chain, provided strong demand for the APX listing.

In the 2010s, PDB invested heavily in expanding its resin production capacity at Badia Curta, more than doubling output to meet growing global demand. The company also began producing specialised engineering plastics and compounds for the automotive and electronics industries, moving up the value chain from basic commodity resins. By the mid-2020s, PDB had become one of the world's largest plastics and petrochemicals companies, with exports to over 40 countries and operations across the BTPMEU.

The company has faced criticism from environmental groups for its heavy reliance on petrochemical feedstocks and for the environmental impact of its production processes, particularly in the Badia Curta area, where the concentration of CIEA's refining and PDB's resin production has created significant industrial pollution. The company has responded with environmental improvement programmes and investments in recycling capacity, though critics argue these have been insufficient relative to the scale of the company's operations.

== Operations ==

PDB operates production facilities across the BTPMEU:

* '''Badia Curta complex''' (Balboa): The company's original and largest production site, adjacent to CIEA's Badia Curta refinery. Resin production facilities connected by pipeline to the refinery for direct feedstock supply, producing polyethylene, polypropylene, PVC, PET, and specialised engineering plastics. The complex also houses the company's research and development division. Approximately 4,000 employees.

* '''Sant Cristòfor manufacturing''' (Balboa): Facilities in the Dàrsena district producing finished plastic products — packaging, injection-moulded components, and industrial plastics — for the domestic market and for export through the Sant Cristòfor harbour. Approximately 3,000 employees.

* '''Tapuya manufacturing''': Packaging and component manufacturing facilities acquired in the early 2000s, producing finished plastic products for the Tapuyan market and for export. Lower labour costs in Tapuya make these facilities more cost-competitive for labour-intensive manufacturing. Approximately 7,000 employees.

* '''Potocsí manufacturing''': Packaging and industrial plastics facilities serving the Potocsí market. Approximately 4,000 employees.

The company's four main product lines are:

* '''Plastic resins''': Basic and specialised resins sold to manufacturers worldwide. The negative tariff makes these exports competitive despite the overvalued ID, and the company's low feedstock and energy costs give it a structural cost advantage. Resins account for approximately 40 per cent of revenue and are the company's largest export category, shipped through the Sant Cristòfor harbour to manufacturers across Illypnia, Kaftia, and beyond.
* '''Plastic packaging''': Bottles, containers, and films for the processed food industry, including [[Hirocorp]] and other food processors across the BTPMEU. Sold primarily to BTPMEU markets behind the common external tariff. Packaging accounts for approximately 30 per cent of revenue.
* '''Plastic components''': Injection-moulded parts for the assembly sector — casings, housings, fittings, and structural components. These feed into the assembly model, reducing dependence on imported components from [[Sinchew]] and [[Tapuya]], and are also exported directly to Illypnian manufacturers. Components account for approximately 20 per cent of revenue.
* '''Industrial plastics''': Pipes, sheets, and construction materials for the domestic and BTPMEU construction markets. Industrial plastics account for approximately 10 per cent of revenue.

PDB sources its petrochemical feedstocks — naphtha, ethylene, propylene — from CIEA's Badia Curta refinery under a long-term supply agreement. CIEA's 12% stake in PDB reflects the strategic importance of this relationship. The overvalued ID works in PDB's favour on the input side: CIEA imports crude oil cheaply (strong currency), refines it, and supplies feedstocks to PDB at competitive prices, which PDB then converts into plastic products for export with the negative tariff. This vertical chain — cheap crude in, competitive plastic products out — is a direct consequence of the currency overvaluation that otherwise disadvantages Balboan manufacturing.
